<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phan Châu Trinh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Phan Chu Trinh, Phan Chau Trinh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phan Châu Trinh was a Vietnamese reformer who argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> should modernise through education, commerce and legal reform within French rule rather than through armed revolt. He helped found the Đông Kinh Free School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> in 1907 and was imprisoned on Côn Sơn island after the tax protests of 1908. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1911 to 1925, and his funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> drew huge crowds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phan-chau-trinh.jpg" alt="Phan Châu Trinh" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khánh Ký · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phan_Chau_Trinh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Phan Châu Trinh was born in Tây Lộc village in Quảng Nam province, the third son of a wealthy scholar who became an official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/can-vuong">Cần Vương</a> association of Quảng Nam in 1885 and was killed by its other leaders on suspicion in 1887. Phan returned to Confucian study, obtained the Cử nhân degree in the regional examination of 1900 and the Phó bảng title in the national examination a year later, and in 1903 became a mid-ranking official of the Ministry of Rites. He resigned in 1905, having turned against the monarchy and the mandarin system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1906 Phan travelled, disguised as a labourer, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> to meet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phan-boi-chau">Phan Bội Châu</a>, and went with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as part of the Đông-Du movement. The two argued for weeks. Phan Bội Châu wanted to drive out the French first and to make use of the monarchy, while Phan Châu Trinh, who distrusted Japanese militarism, wanted to abolish the monarchy and make use of the French in the quest for progress. Back home he led the Duy Tân Movement with Huỳnh Thúc Kháng and Trần Quý Cáp, and late in 1906 he wrote to the governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>, Paul Beau, calling on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to build modern legal, educational and economic institutions.</p><p>At the Tonkin Free School, opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> in 1907 with Lương Văn Can and Nguyễn Quyền, he lectured from translated works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and Liang Qichao. Arrested after the peasant tax revolts of 1908, he was sentenced to death, a sentence commuted to life imprisonment after admirers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> intervened. Pardoned in 1911 and offered house arrest, he chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> to France. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 1915 he worked with Phan Văn Trường, Nguyễn An Ninh, Nguyễn Tất Thành and Nguyễn Thế Truyền in the Group of Vietnamese Patriots, whose articles were signed Nguyễn Ái Quốc, a name Hồ Chí Minh later used.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Phan returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> in 1925 and died there on 24 March 1926, aged 53. Over 60,000 people attended his funeral, which set off protests across the country demanding an end to French colonial rule. His writings include poems composed in prison in 1908 and in the Santé prison from 1914 to 1915, and an account of the unrest of 1908. A temple built in 1933 near his tomb in Tân Bình, Saigon, is part of a memorial site of 2,500 square metres that became a national relic in 1994, and most cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> have major streets named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phan Châu Trinh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most important, colonial rule produced the movements that ended it: reformers such as Phan Boi Chau and <b>Phan Chau Trinh</b>, the Vietnamese Nationalist Party and above all the communist movement of Nguyen Ai Quoc (Ho Chi Minh), who founded the Vietnamese Communist Party in Hong Kong in February…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tam Kỳ, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Ho Chi Minh City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.57,108.48&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Tam Kỳ on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7510 1585 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Phan Châu Trinh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7960 1855h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7960 1855h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15.57,108.48&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
